WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.029
This video covers the small microcontrollers inside conferencing accessories: real time operating systems, a classic bug called priority inversion, and the TinyUSB stack.

00:00:11.429 --> 00:00:15.319
MCU: Microcontroller: a small, low-power chip with its own memory, running firmware without a full operating system. Like: A pocket calculator compared with a laptop.

00:00:15.619 --> 00:00:24.106
TinyUSB: A small open-source USB stack used on microcontrollers. Like: A pocket phrasebook for USB instead of the full dictionary.

00:00:25.206 --> 00:00:28.828
The extension microphone pod on the table has no Linux at all.

00:00:29.078 --> 00:00:33.211
It runs a tiny chip with a scheduler that must answer within microseconds.

00:00:33.461 --> 00:00:35.597
Let's look inside that smaller world.

00:00:36.647 --> 00:00:43.590
An RTOS scheduler is an emergency room triage nurse. Every tick, the most urgent ready patient is seen.

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:49.145
RTOS: Real-time operating system: a tiny scheduler that guarantees tasks run within deadlines. Like: A strict school timetable: every class starts exactly on the bell.

00:00:49.445 --> 00:00:52.603
Now a heart attack patient needs the only stretcher.

00:00:53.003 --> 00:01:02.570
But a sprained ankle patient is holding it, and flu patients, more urgent than the ankle, keep getting seen first. The heart attack waits. That's priority inversion.

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:11.770
The fix: while the ankle patient holds the stretcher, treat them as top priority, so they return it fast. That's priority inheritance.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:13.667
Words.

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:20.324
An RTOS is a real time operating system, like Free R T O S, Zephyr or ThreadX.

00:01:20.724 --> 00:01:24.393
The tick is a periodic timer interrupt that drives scheduling.

00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:34.905
IRQ / interrupt: Interrupt request: a hardware signal that makes the processor stop and handle an event right now. Like: A doorbell: you stop what you are doing and answer it.

00:01:35.205 --> 00:01:41.451
Priority inheritance temporarily raises a task holding a mutex to the priority of the task waiting for it.

00:01:41.851 --> 00:01:50.466
Spinlock / mutex: Locks that stop two pieces of code changing the same data at once; a spinlock waits busily, a mutex sleeps. Like: A single bathroom key: a spinlock is waiting at the door, a mutex is taking a seat until called.

00:01:50.716 --> 00:01:54.373
A watchdog resets the chip unless software kicks it regularly.

00:01:54.773 --> 00:01:59.081
And TinyUSB is an open source USB stack for microcontrollers.

00:02:00.281 --> 00:02:03.450
Here's lab ten's simulation, step by step.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:07.530
At tick zero, the low priority logger takes a mutex.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:13.108
At tick one, the high priority USB task wants the same mutex, and blocks.

00:02:13.508 --> 00:02:22.274
From tick two, the medium task becomes ready. It outranks low, so it runs for six ticks. Low can't finish, so it can't release the mutex.

00:02:22.674 --> 00:02:28.015
The high priority task is stuck behind a medium one, and finishes only at tick eleven.

00:02:28.415 --> 00:02:33.616
Now run it again with inheritance. When high blocks, low is boosted to high priority.

00:02:34.016 --> 00:02:38.637
Medium can't preempt it, so low finishes quickly and releases the mutex.

00:02:39.037 --> 00:02:46.606
High runs at once, and finishes at tick five instead of eleven. That's the bug that nearly sank the Mars Pathfinder mission in nineteen ninety seven.

00:02:47.806 --> 00:02:51.905
A TinyUSB device in a few lines. Initialize the stack.

00:02:52.305 --> 00:03:01.686
Call t u d task in the main loop or an RTOS task. The interrupt only records events; this function handles them. Same pattern as lesson two.

00:03:02.086 --> 00:03:04.895
The stack calls you back for the device descriptor.

00:03:05.295 --> 00:03:08.534
Descriptor: A small table of facts the device reports about itself: who made it, what it can do. Like: A business card plus resume the device hands over when it is first plugged in.

00:03:08.784 --> 00:03:11.002
And for the configuration descriptor.

00:03:11.402 --> 00:03:16.789
Class callbacks deliver data, here an H I D output report that sets the mute LED.

00:03:17.189 --> 00:03:30.598
HID: Human Interface Device: the standard language for buttons, keyboards and mice, also used for mute and call buttons. Like: The universal remote-control language: any computer knows what "button pressed" means.

00:03:31.698 --> 00:03:33.219
Bare metal essentials.

00:03:33.619 --> 00:03:40.156
At reset, startup code copies initialized data, zeroes the rest, sets clocks, and calls main.

00:03:40.556 --> 00:03:43.737
Size every task's stack, and check the high water mark.

00:03:44.137 --> 00:03:47.330
Kick the watchdog only when every task reports healthy.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:54.672
On a Cortex M hard fault, read the fault status register, the fault address registers, and the stacked program counter.

00:03:55.072 --> 00:03:58.834
And debug over S W D with Open O C D and gdb.

00:03:59.234 --> 00:04:08.127
JTAG / SWD: Hardware debug ports that let a debugger stop the processor and read its memory. Like: A doctor's stethoscope plugged straight into the chip.

00:04:09.227 --> 00:04:17.110
Question for you: A high-priority task waits on a lock held by a low-priority task. What goes wrong, and what is the fix?

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:35.948
Answer: A medium task can keep the low one from running, so the high one waits too long. That is priority inversion. Priority inheritance lends the low task the high priority while it holds the lock.

00:04:37.048 --> 00:04:37.744
Recap.

00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:40.664
The highest priority ready task runs.

00:04:41.064 --> 00:04:43.908
Use mutexes with priority inheritance.

00:04:44.308 --> 00:04:46.781
Interrupts record, tasks process.

00:04:47.181 --> 00:04:49.329
Use watchdogs and stack checks.

00:04:49.729 --> 00:04:52.318
And the fault registers point straight at the bug.
